[metadata][%
  title: Turn
  author: ...
  date: [today]
  theme: light
%]

[slide][%Purpose%]
- Turn is for defining situations.
- It weaves queries, action, and choice together over time

[slide][%Why a Language%]
- Old definitions should rarely need to change to accommodate new ones
  - new attributes of old entities
  - new behaviors within old ones
  - *avoid fixed local structure*
- Matching the efficiency of natural language
  - *avoid encodings

[slide][%Atoms%]
The basic constituent of a situation is called a *tuple* or *atom*
[code][%
turn T                   -- "T is a turn"
card C                   -- "C is a card"
card:name C pot-of-greed -- "the name of C is pot-of-greed"
%]
[pause]
(variables are capitalized; symbols lower-case)

[slide][%The Now%]
A compound query is a sequence of atoms. It describes an interval in time, the *now*,
reading from top to bottom:
[code][%
turn T       -- during some Turn... [pause]
play-card C  -- a card is played... [pause]
action C     -- that is an action... [pause]
~activate C  -- so activate the card.
%]

[slide][%Kinds of Action%]
- [%~episode%]: creates a new interval contained within *now*
- [%+fact%]: creates a new interval (left, Top), with left contained in *now*, extending indefinitely forward in time
- [%^property%]: creates a new interval equal to *now*

[slide][%Triggering the Rule%]
[code][%
-- Reminder: the rule we want to trigger
-- turn T, play-card C, action C, ~activate C
[pause]
+action C     -- create an action card (fact) [pause]
~turn T       -- create a Turn (after `action` begins) (episode) [pause]
~play-card C  -- play the card (during that turn) (episode)

-- re-introduce the rule... [pause]
turn T, play-card C, action C, ~activate C
%][timeline]

[slide][%Timeline View%]
[code][%
~game

game, ~turn

turn
  ( ~action-phase );
  ( ~buy-phase );
  ( ~cleanup-phase )
%][timeline]

[slide][%The now(s)%]
Sometimes we need several related points in time, so we allow grouping.
This temporarily shrinks the interval and later pops the changes:
[code][%
-- "If you play the card you drew, ... [pause]
turn T
  ( draw-phase, draw-card C )    -- "drew C" [pause]
  ( action-phase, play-card C )  -- "play C"
  -- ...
%]
[pause]
The two phases do not overlap, but we can still match this situation after it happened and respond.

[slide][%"Co-routines" everywhere%]
- a rule is a sequence of matches or assertions:
  - [%r := (m | a)*%] ... [pause]
- but the structure of this equivalent regex is more important:
  - [%r := (m* a)*%] ...[pause]
- because we split each rule into a sequence of [%m* a%]'s (a sequence of matches followed by one assert). [pause]
- If you think of an [%r%] as a synchronous sequence of reads and writes, then a single [%m* a%] is a write that yields

[slide][%Why "Co-routines" everywhere?%]
Step 1: assert [%b%] and [%d%] using the rule:
[code][%
a, ~b, c, ~d
%][timeline] [pause]
(this slide is a live demo)

[slide][%Why "Co-routines" everywhere?%]
Step 2: assert [%b%] and [%d%] using the rules:
[code][%
a, ~b

b, c, ~d
%][timeline] [pause]
(this slide is a live demo)

[slide][%"Co-routines" everywhere%]
- The split is semantics preserving at the individual rule level
  - [%a, ~b, c, ~d%] -> [%a, ~b%] + [%b, c, ~d%]
  - We ensure that the match on [%b%] matches *only* the locally asserted [%b%] (not any other [%b%] from another rule)

[slide][%Why "Co-routines" everywhere?%]
*When you activate *dream*, gain a power.
If you forget dream, play the gained power.[pause]
[code][%
activate It, card:name It dream
  ~gain-power
    (gain New)
    (forget It)
  ~play-power New
[pause]
gain-power
  ~forget-power;
  ~choose-new-power
[pause]
forget-power
  ? P, ~forget P, !power P
[pause]
choose-new-power
  ? P, +gain P, !power P
%]
[pause]
- [%activate%] treats [%forget%] and [%gain%] like return values, but they don't need to be declared as such. [pause]
- execution of [%activate%] "pauses" while rules matching [%gain-power%] resolve [pause]
  - a prefix of the rule asserts [%gain-power%]; then a suffix matches that tuple along with its associated [%gain%] and [%forget%] before "continuing" [pause]
  - *these things are all semantically datalog rules*

[slide][%Aggregates%]
- We often need to reduce over a relation
  - (*your score is the sum of ...*, *if you gained no cards this turn, ...*)
- Aggregates are demand-driven and calculated at some interval [pause]
- *We use aggregates to implement state.*

[slide][%Where am I?%]
- Answer: wherever you last put me.[pause]

- variables and physical tokens are logically equivalent
  - a variable is a slot that provides access to the value it was most recently set to
  - a token is an entity that is located at the place it was most recently moved [pause]

[code][%
#acc at * -> last

-- Explanation:[pause]
-- #acc ...          -- Directive declaring an accumulated relation[pause]
-- #acc at ...       -- named *at*[pause]
-- #acc at * ...     -- taking one argument of any type[pause]
-- #acc at * -> last -- aggregated by *last* (?)
%]

[slide][%last%]
- [%last(a, b) := b%]
- last is applied to values in temporal order, so it evaluates to the most recent one

[slide][%Aggregation: Last Position%]
[code][%
#acc at * -> last

~game
  ( ~step here );
  ( ~look );
  ( ~step there );
  ( ~look );
[pause]
-- We use `step` to move `me` around...
step L, +at me -> L
[pause]
-- and `look` to observe its current location.
look, at me -> L, ~print L
%][timeline]

[slide][%Choices%]
- A rule set is compiled into a monotone logic program.
- Given a set of past decision tuples, this LP computes a set of choices to be made.
- The choice set is represented by a query.
  - The query, evaluated at the right *now*, matches valid choices.

[slide][%Choices%]
[code][%
choose-card
  ?Card      -- introduces *choice variable*
  ~it Card   -- a handle for other queries to grab onto
  !card Card -- asserts that any valid decision must be a `card`
%]

[slide][%Composable Choice%]
Other rules can independently refine the choice:
[code][%
choose-card
  ?Card      -- introduces *choice variable*
  ~it Card   -- allows other queries to grab it
  !card Card -- asserts that any valid decision must be a `card`
[pause]
-- If *Earthquake* is active:
--   when choosing a card, it must be *sturdy*. [pause]

earthquake, choose-card, it C
  !sturdy C
%]
[pause]
... that's it.

[slide][%Composable Choice%]
There might be multiple ways for a card to be *sturdy*:
[code][%
+card shelter
+sturdy shelter  -- it is a fact that Shelter is sturdy
[pause]
-- "If at least two cards have been
-- played this turn, *Trading Post* is sturdy
turn
  ( play-card _, play-card-count -> N, le 2 N )
  ^sturdy trading-post
%]
